AB  - Acid mine drainage (AMD) poses a significant environmental challenge associated with mining sulphide-rich minerals, resulting in the pollution of surface and groundwater. Water discharged from active, abandoned, and reclaimed mine sites, often characterized by elevated acidity, remains a global concern due to its variable impacts on water quality. This study evaluates the capability of Sentinel-2 Global Mosaic data (S2GM-2) to map AMD in the Rio Tinto region of Spain, an area severely impacted by long-term mining activities and consequent water pollution, covering an analyzed area of 15,700 km2. Two input data scenarios were utilized for subsequent machine learning classifications using Support Vector Machine (SVM) and Random Forest (RF) algorithms: i) cloudless S-2 reflectance and ii) S-2 derived indices. The classifications were validated using freely available datasets, including a geochemical dataset from the USGS and the global mine footprint layer. The Random Forest (RF) classifier successfully differentiated 5 technosol classes and 3 water pollution categories, whereas Support Vector Machine (SVM) classification identified only 2 technosol and 2 water pollution classes To conclude the RF classification outperformed SVM, moreover using S-2 derived indices as input data enabled effective mapping of mine water pollution transport from the source to the lower reaches of the Rio Tinto and Odiel rivers. This demonstration effectively showcases the future potential of this approach as a foundation for the upcoming AMD monitoring service.
